  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 248 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 mid level water vapor imagery shows deep dry \nlayer overhead associated with mid/upper lvl ridge, while closed \ncyclonic circulations lingers off the mid Atlantic Coast. As sfc low \npres moves slowly northward the pres gradient btwn low and high pres \nincreases late Thurs into Friday, for a period of elevated winds. In \naddition, this wl help push a weak cold acrs our cwa on Thurs aftn, \nwith slightly cooler temps on Friday. \n\nProgged 925mb temps range btwn 8-10C this aftn, combined with plenty \nof sunshine supports highs mid 50s to lower 60s, very similar to \nTuesday. Lows tonight wl be highly depend on elevation as a sharp \nllvl thermal inversion develops with poor rh recoveries possible \nacrs the mid-upper elevations within this thermal belt. Lows wl \nrange from the mid 20s to mid/upper 30s with a sharp humidity range \nfrom excellent recoveries in the valleys to poor acrs the ridges. As \nthe boundary approaches on Thurs, did note a slight increase in \n925mb to 850mb moisture, along with a strengthening low level jet of \n20 to 35 knots. Strongest winds appear to occur btwn 00z and 12z \nFriday, when mixing profiles are less favorable, but expect \nlocalized gusty winds on ridges and Lake Champlain. Once again with \ndeveloping winds poor rh recoveries are possible, especially summit \nlvl on Friday morning with a large spread in temps anticipated. \nFriday is the coolest day of the week with highs upper 30s to lower \n50s acrs our region, did utilize the max temp by elevation tool to \nhighlight sharpening gradient btwn warmer valleys and cooler \nsummits. Fire weather concerns increase Thursday aftn into Friday \nwith slightly stronger surface winds, near critically dry humidities \nand recent dry spell resulting in receptive fine fuels, especially \nleaf litter. Winds and humidities look to fall short of red flag \nconditions, but we wl continue to monitor closely in the upcoming \ndays ahead.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2:  Surface high pressure will remain the dominant feature \nSunday into Monday, leading to a continuation of quiet weather. Late \nMonday into Tuesday, a front will drop south out of Canada, though \nthe frontal passage will once again be largely uneventful.  A few \nisolated light showers or mountain flurries are possible as the \nfront moves through, however lack of deep moisture will be a \nlimiting factor for precipitation potential. Temperatures will trend \ndownward behind the front, dropping to slightly below normal by \nmidweek.",
